<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet>
  <title>Exam 3 - Math 325</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      For each of the following problems, you will state the solution of the problem as a theorem, and you will write a proof of the theorem.  Keep the following in mind.
      <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                All proofs must be written in complete sentences.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                If computations are a part of your proof, they must be written as part of the proof. Do not separate the computations from the explanations.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Your proof may not have any unproved steps.  All steps must be justified by definitions, your previously proven steps, or by facts we proved in class or in videos.
            </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (30 pts.) Let <m>G</m> be a connected graph that contains a cycle <m>v_1v_2 \ldots v_nv_1</m>.  Prove that if you remove one of the edges in the cycle, the resulting graph is connected.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>T</m> be a tree with at least two vertices, and consider the following algorithm applied to <m>T</m>.
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Choose a random vertex in <m>T</m>.  Call it current_vertex.
        
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If possible, choose a vertex that is adjacent to current_vertex that has not been visited yet; call this new vertex adjacent_vertex.  If it is not possible, stop the algorithm and return the vertex current_vertex.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If the algorithm has not yet been stopped, change the name of adjacent_vertex to current_vertex and repeat step (2) above.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            Prove that the algorithm eventually terminates and that the output of the algorithm is a leaf (i.e. a vertex of degree 1).  Hint:  I suggest letting <m>v_1, v_2, \ldots , v_n</m> be the vertices that are visited (in order), where <m>v_n</m> is the output of the algorithm.  I would suggest a proof by contradiction.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            (40 pts.) Choose one of the following.  You will likely need to use the theorem you proved in #1 on this exam in your proof.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>T</m> be a tree you get from Kruskal's algorithm, and let <m>\{ e_1, e_2, \ldots , e_r\}</m> be the edges of <m>T</m> listed in the order you choose them in Kruskal's algorithm.  Let <m>T_m,</m> be a minimum spanning tree, and let <m>e_k</m> be the first edge of <m>T</m> that is not in <m>T_m</m> (if such an edge exists).
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Prove that adding <m>e_k</m> to <m>T_m</m> creates a cycle.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Prove that there is some edge <m>e</m> in <m>T_m</m> but not in <m>T</m> such that if you remove <m>e</m> and replace it with <m>e_k</m>, the resulting graph is a spanning tree.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Prove that <m>w(e_k) \geq w(e)</m>.  Hint: <m>T_m</m> is a minimum spanning tree, so what does that tell you about the spanning tree from (b)?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Prove that <m>w(e_k) \leq w(e)</m>.  Hint: At the <m>k^{\hbox{th}}</m> step of Kruskal's algorithm, would it be appropriate to choose <m>e</m> instead of <m>e_k</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens to the weight of <m>T_m</m> if you replace <m>e</m> with <m>e_k</m>?  Explain how you would continue this process to show <m>T</m> is a minimum spanning tree.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </task>
    <task>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>T</m> be a tree you get from Prim's algorithm, and let <m>\{ e_1, e_2, \ldots , e_r\}</m> be the edges of <m>T</m> listed in the order you choose them in Prim's algorithm.  Let <m>T_m,</m> be a minimum spanning tree
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose <m>e_1</m> is not in <m>T</m>.  Prove that adding <m>e_1</m> to <m>T_m</m> creates a cycle.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Prove that <m>e_1</m> is in a minimum spanning tree.  Hint: Remove a carefully chosen edge from the cycle you created in (a).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Using a similar technique as in (a) and (b), prove that <m>e_1</m> and <m>e_2</m> are part of a common minimum spanning tree.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Prove that <m>e_1</m>, <m>e_2</m>, and <m>e_3</m> are part of a common minimum spanning tree.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Prove that all <m>e_i</m> form a minimum spanning tree by induction.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>

        </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>